## Why This Question Is Asked
This problem evaluates a candidate's ability to construct precise 3-valued assignments with exact unknown counts under Kleene semantics, along with a practical backtracking strategy for constraint satisfaction.

## Key Concepts
- Kleene 3-valued logic semantics
- Exact unknown count in constraint satisfaction
- Propagation and backtracking in three-valued logic

## Code Example
```javascript
function not(v) {
  return v === 'T' ? 'F' : v === 'F' ? 'T' : 'U';
}

function imp(a, b) {
  return not(a) === 'T' ? b : not(a);
}

function evaluate(expr, vars) {
  if (Array.isArray(expr)) {
    const [op, left, right] = expr;
    const l = evaluate(left, vars);
    const r = evaluate(right, vars);
    if (op === 'and') return min(l, r);
    if (op === 'or') return max(l, r);
    if (op === 'imp') return imp(l, r);
  }
  return vars[expr];
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a tiny 3-valued solver: propagation, clash detection, and controlled backtracking while preserving non-boolean placeholders.

## Key Concepts
- 3-valued logic propagation
- Forward-checking and fixpoint computation
- Backtracking with Unknowns to preserve flexibility

## Code Example
```javascript
function propagate(vars, constraints) {
  // vars: {S1: T|F|U, S2: T|F|U, S3: T|F|U}
  // constraints: list of functions returning {changed, ok}
  // Minimal sketch for illustration
  let changed = true;
  while (changed) {
    changed = false;
    for (const c of constraints) {
      const res = c(vars);
      if (res.changed) changed = true;
      if (!res.ok) return {ok: false};
    }
  }
  return {ok: true, vars};
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to reason with Kleene three-valued logic in a small constraint system and to design backtracking propagation that respects non-classical truth tables.

## Key Concepts

- Kleene three-valued logic with T>U>F
- Implication defined as ¬a ∨ b
- Propagation and backtracking in 3-valued CSPs

## Code Example

```javascript
function neg(x){ return x==="T"?"F": x==="F"?"T":"U"; }
function or(a,b){ return (a==="T"||b==="T")?"T": (a==="U"||b==="U")?"U":"F"; }
function and(a,b){ return (a==="F"||b=="F")?"F": (a==="U"||b==="U")?"U":"T"; }
function implies(a,b){ // ¬a ∨ b
  const na = neg(a);
  return or(na,b);
}
```

